% Ostomy Supplies: LCD L33828 and its Policy Article A52487 (DME MACs). A
% policy program of the DMEPOS model: it includes dmepos.le (orders,
% delivery, benefit category, continued need, refills) and states when a
% claim for an ostomy supply — the pouches, barriers, faceplates, pastes,
% belts, irrigation supplies, adhesives, tape and the rest of the LCD's
% table of usual maximum quantities, and the pouches and barriers the
% Article's coding guidelines name — is REASONABLE AND NECESSARY: every
% supply within the usual maximum quantity of the LCD's table (per month,
% or per six months), or beyond it with the greater quantity explained in
% the medical record; not both of two supplies the LCD says are used one
% instead of the other (the liquid barrier A4369 and the wipes or swabs
% A5120; the night drainage bag A4357 and bottle A5102 of a urinary
% ostomy). The benefit is the Prosthetic Device benefit (Policy Article
% A52487, Social Security Act §1861(s)(8)), which carries no condition on
% the place of use.
%
% The payment conditions the Article adds (its "statutory payment policy
% requirements", section remedy): a beneficiary with a permanent impairment
% requiring an ostomy — a colostomy, an ileostomy or a urinary ostomy, with
% a diagnosis code of the Article's ICD-10 list on the claim
% (sources/icd10/A52487_icd10_covered.csv, 10 rows, one group: group 1
% serves every code of the policy; loaded as the table ostomy_icd10); not
% in a covered home health episode (the home health agency provides the
% supplies); an ostomy clamp (A4363) only when ordered as a replacement;
% and the dispensing cap of the LCD's refill paragraph — a one-month supply
% at a time for a beneficiary in a nursing facility, three months at home.
% Once that benefit is met, continued need needs no further documentation
% (the Article's "CONTINUED MEDICAL NEED": an addition to the shared rules
% of dmepos.le; continued use is shown by the refill request, which
% dmepos.le checks).
%
% How the criteria are read. What the record states is a scenario fact:
% the kind of ostomy ("the ostomy of Ann is colostomy": colostomy,
% ileostomy, urinary ostomy), the diagnosis code, the home health episode,
% the residence. The quantities are the LCD's table, as data (the tables
% ostomy_quantity and ostomy_period), read against the quantity billed and
% the months the claim supplies (the shared templates); a code the table
% does not list has no usual maximum. Two judgments are findings, the
% judged template of dmepos.le "the finding F about B is established" (or
% unsupported), with who found it and why:
%   permanent impairment requiring ostomy (the Article: "If the medical
%   record, including the judgment of the treating practitioner, indicates
%   the condition is of long and indefinite duration, the test of permanence
%   is considered met");
%   excess ostomy supplies explained (a quantity above the usual maximum:
%   "The explanation for use of a greater quantity of supplies than the
%   amounts listed must be clearly documented in the beneficiary's medical
%   record"; consulted only when the listed amount is exceeded).
%
% Not modelled: the continent stoma's "No more than one of these types of
% supply would be reasonable and necessary on a given day" (A5055, A5081,
% A5083, A6216 — a daily use no claim states); the Column I / Column II
% bundling of the faceplate systems and the clamps "billed with ostomy
% pouches" (coding); the AU modifier of the tape and adhesives; the pouch
% cover, coded A9270 (the generic noncovered code, which no policy of the
% model decides); A4400, "not valid for claim submission". The codes
% A4364, A4402, A4450, A4452 and A5120 are also in the tracheostomy
% supplies policy, and A4450, A4452 and A6216 are surgical dressings as
% well: the Article's AU modifier tells them apart on a real claim, and in
% a model holding both policies such a claim meets both.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    % the record
    the ostomy of *a beneficiary* is *a kind*; undefined.
    *a beneficiary* is in a covered home health episode; undefined.
    *an item* was ordered as a replacement; undefined.
    % the tables
    ICD10 code *a code* is in group *a group* of article *an article* as *a description* under table ostomy_icd10.
    the usual maximum for ostomy supply *a code* is *a quantity* under table ostomy_quantity.
    the period of the usual maximum for ostomy supply *a code* is *a number* months under table ostomy_period.
    % derived
    *an item* is an ostomy supply.
    *an item* conflicts with a concurrent ostomy supply.
    *a claim* is within the usual maximum quantity of ostomy supplies.
    *a claim* is within the listed amount of ostomy supplies.
    the ostomy supply quantity of *a claim* is allowed for *a beneficiary*.
    *a beneficiary* meets the ostomy benefit requirements.
    the diagnosis code of *a beneficiary* is a covered ostomy diagnosis code.
    the ostomy clamp requirement is met for *an item*.
    *a claim* is within the dispensing limit for ostomy supplies.

% The Policy Article's ICD-10 table: the covered codes of its one group.
the table ostomy_icd10 is loaded from sources/icd10/A52487_icd10_covered.csv, with all matches:
    code | group | article | description

